A||||2||fierospeeder||GM gas tank question||||1||
Z||000000||fierospeeder||09-11-2000||06:26 PM||||My mom owns a 88 chevy celebrity station wagon, which we are going to sell for 1500, if anyone wants it in illinois.<P>Anway, when the gas tank is 1/4 full or less, i notice gas isn't being sucked up when im doing a 90 degree turn into a side street, the car wont accelerate until the gas stabalizes in the tank.  Slamming on the brake sometimes makes the car die.  I was wondering if these cars are suppose to do this, or is the pickup loose or something?  The car works fine when its 1/2 or more filled with gas. <P>thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||Gold-86SE||09-12-2000||09:18 AM||||<BR>There is a filter screen on the end of the fuel pump.  That screen may be caked with petroleum crud, and this may be causing your problems, or the fuel pump itself is starting to go, or the fuel filter is bad.<P>I'd start with a new fuel filter and see if that helps with the problem.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Gold-86SE (Fiero Collector)||||reg||1
Z||000002||HugGRRRFiero||09-12-2000||09:45 AM||||I agree with Gold-86SE.  The pickup for the pump should always stay at the bottom of the tank.  There are springs that expand and contract to hold the sender unit down in the tank, they could have broke, but I doubt it.<P>Doesn't suprise me, when I try to make turns in my '85 Regal like I do with my Fiero the gas gauge can fluctuate as much as a 1/4 to 1/3 of a tank!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Of course my Regal is supposed to handle better than the Fiero according to a recent Automobile article.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/huggrrr.gif">    <BR>    <A HREF="mailto:HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com">HugGRRRFiero@hotmail.com</A>  <p>[This message has been edited by HugGRRRFiero (edited 09-12-2000).]||||reg||1
